  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100888,
      "id": "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a",
      "title": "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b1b891c9-2e98-5828-854d-903481ea1a27",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=8 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}},\\qquad 2 x - 2>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=4 \\ln{\\left(2 x - 2 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{8}{2 x - 2}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=4 \\ln{\\left(2 x - 2 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(4 \\ln{\\left(2 x - 2 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=4\\cdot\\frac{2}{2 x - 2} \\\\ &=\\frac{8}{2 x - 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4 \\ln{\\left(2 x - 2 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{8}{2 x - 2}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}8 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{2 x - 2} &\\overset{?}{=}8e^{-(4 \\ln{\\left(2 x - 2 \\right)})/(4)} \\\\ \\frac{8}{2 x - 2} &\\overset{?}{=}8e^{-\\ln(2 x - 2)} \\\\ \\frac{8}{2 x - 2} &=\\frac{4}{x - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4 \\ln{\\left(2 x - 2 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
